According to the documentation, `updateWithJS` can be slow for large tables because it involves transferring data between DuckDB and JavaScript. Here's a simple example:
```
import { SimpleDB } from "@nshiab/simple-data-analysis";
// We start a SimpleDB instance
const sdb = new SimpleDB();
// We create a new table
const table = sdb.newTable("myTable");
// Load some data
await table.loadData("./my-data.csv");
// Update the data
await table.updateWithJS((rows) => {
  const modifiedRows = rows.map((d) => ({
    ...d,
    column1: typeof d.column1 === "number" ? d.column1 + 1 : null,
  }));
  return modifiedRows;
});
// Log the updated table
await table.logTable();
// We close everything
await sdb.done();
```

